  "id": "COMPOUND:33519 - PROTEIN:585",
  "verbalization": "The visible evidence suggests that Acetic Acid Isobutyl Ester may interact with Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase through multiple indirect pathways. The first pathway links Acetic Acid Isobutyl Ester to Inosine-5'-monophosphate dehydrogenase 1, which is subsequently targeted by a complex molecule, (2R,3R,4S,4aR,6aR,6bS,14aR,14bR)-2,3-dihydroxy-4,6a,6b,11,12,14b-hexamethyl-1,2,3,4a,5,6,7,8,14,14a-decahydropicene-4-carboxylic acid, which in turn targets Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase. The second pathway connects Acetic Acid Isobutyl Ester to L-lactate dehydrogenase C chain, which is targeted by 7-Xylosyltaxol, a compound that also targets Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase. The third pathway similarly links Acetic Acid Isobutyl Ester to Inosine-5'-monophosphate dehydrogenase 1, which is targeted by Stereonsteroid D, which also targets Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase. These findings collectively suggest that Acetic Acid Isobutyl Ester may act as a modulator or inhibitor of Glyceraldehyde-3-phosphate dehydrogenase through various biochemical pathways, though the exact mechanism of interaction remains to be fully elucidated.",
